     Standard C Library (libc, -lc)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<sseemmaapphhoorree..hh>>

     _i_n_t
     sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee(_s_e_m___t _* _r_e_s_t_r_i_c_t _s_e_m, _i_n_t _* _r_e_s_t_r_i_c_t _s_v_a_l);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee() function sets the variable pointed to by _s_v_a_l to the
     current value of the semaphore pointed to by _s_e_m, as of the time that the
     call to sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee() is actually run.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee() function returns the value 0 if successful; otherwise
     the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate
     the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee() function will fail if:

     [EINVAL]           The _s_e_m argument points to an invalid semaphore.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     sem_post(3), sem_trywait(3), sem_wait(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The sseemm__ggeettvvaalluuee() function conforms to ISO/IEC 9945-1:1996 (“POSIX.1”).

     The value of the semaphore is never negative, even if there are threads
     blocked on the semaphore.  POSIX is somewhat ambiguous in its wording
     with regard to what the value of the semaphore should be if there are
     blocked waiting threads, but this behavior is conformant, given the word‐
     ing of the specification.

